            0.105
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Homi",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.204,
            0.105,
            0.312,
            0.197
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Jehangir Bhabha",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " was an Indian physicist. He is known as the"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.062,
            0.197,
            0.312,
            0.414
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "father of the Indian nuclear programme. He made a pioneering contribution to the development of atomic energy in India. He established key institutions like the Tata Institute of Fundamental Research (TIFR) and the Bhabha Atomic Research Centre (BARC), for peaceful uses of atomic energy to generate electricity, support agriculture, and advanced medical treatments."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.025,
            0.433,
            0.09,
            0.5
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 5,
              "bbox": [
                0.025,
                0.433,
                0.09,
                0.5
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_16_image_5.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.093,
            0.453,
            0.3,
            0.472
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Ready to Go Beyond"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.087,
            0.478,
            0.302,
            0.679
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Electron microscopes, such as Scanning Tunnelling Microscopes (STMs) and Transmission Electron Microscopes (TEMs) can produce images of materials with atomic-level details. STMs mainly study surfaces (Fig. 8.15), while TEMs reveal how atoms are arranged inside very thin samples."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.081,
            0.683,
            0.3,
            0.798
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 8,
              "bbox": [
                0.081,
                0.683,
                0.3,
                0.798
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_16_image_8.jpg",
              "content": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "image_footnote",
              "index": 9,
              "bbox": [
                0.084,
                0.802,
                0.299,
                0.831
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 8.15: STM image showing individual atoms on a surface"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.342,
            0.088,
            0.889,
            0.173
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "This does not mean that any one atom of chlorine has a fractional mass of 35.5 u. It means that if you take, say 1 million chlorine atoms, they will contain 7.5 lakh "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "^ { 3 5 } _ { 1 7 } \\dot { \\mathrm { C l } }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and 2.5 lakh "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "^ { 3 7 } _ { 1 7 } \\mathsf { C I }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " atoms with a weighted average atomic mass of 35.5 u."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.342,
            0.176,
            0.889,
            0.228
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "This comparison shows that the simple average ignores abundance, whereas the weighted average accurately reflects the mass of the element as it occurs in nature."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.346,
            0.234,
            0.406,
            0.265
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 12,
              "bbox": [
                0.346,
                0.234,
                0.406,
                0.265
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_16_image_12.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.412,
            0.242,
            0.601,
            0.26
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Pause and Ponder"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.375,
            0.272,
            0.869,
            0.335
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "17. Two different atoms have 11 protons each, but one has 12 neutrons, and the other has 13 neutrons. How do their atomic numbers and mass numbers compare? Are they the same element or different elements?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.375,
            0.337,
            0.868,
            0.388
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "18. If a bromine atom is available in the form of, say two isotopes, "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "^ { 7 9 } _ { 3 5 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Br (49.7%) and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "^ { 8 1 } _ { 3 5 } \\mathrm { B r }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " (50.3%), calculate the average atomic mass of the bromine atom."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.343,
            0.417,
            0.89,
            0.503
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "You have learnt that the two atoms that have the same atomic number but different mass numbers are called isotopes. What if they have the same mass number but different atomic numbers? What are such atoms called? Let us find out!"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.344,
            0.511,
            0.49,
            0.531
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "8.9.2 Isobars"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 18,
          "bbox": [
            0.342,
            0.534,
            0.89,
            0.67
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Let us consider three elements — calcium (atomic number 20), potassium (atomic number 19), and argon (atomic number 18). These elements have different numbers of protons, yet each has a mass number of 40. This shows that the total number of nucleons in their atoms is the same, even though they are different elements. When atoms of different elements have the same mass number, but different atomic numbers, they are called "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "isobars",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 19,
          "bbox": [
            0.342,
            0.672,
            0.89,
            0.808
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "As we end our journey into the structure of the atom, it is important to know that the story does not end here. The exploration of the structure of the atom is still being discovered (Fig. 8.16). Later, scientists discovered that even Bohr’s model was not entirely correct. Electrons do not follow well-defined paths like the fixed Bohr orbits. Today, we understand that they exist as ʻelectron cloudsʼ around the nucleus. We can predict regions where they are most"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 20,
          "bbox": [
            0.342,
            0.811,
            0.392,
            0.848
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 20,
              "bbox": [
                0.342,
                0.811,
                0.392,
                0.848
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_16_image_20.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 21,
          "bbox": [
            0.393,
            0.807,
            0.89,
            0.858
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "likely to be, not exactly where they are. You will learn all these details in higher grades. The journey of exploring the mysteries of the atom is far from over,"
            }
          ],
          "continues_prev": true
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 22,
          "bbox": [
            0.344,
            0.857,
            0.7,
            0.875
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "and exciting discoveries still lie ahead!"
            }
          ],
          "continues_prev": true
        },
